{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And I will set My face against that man.</b> The Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote: you will not find in the entire Torah, in any instance of karet, that the word 'I' (אני) is mentioned except in the karet of Molech, where it says 'ואני אתן את פני' — and I will set My face — and 'ושמתי אני את פני' — and I will set My face. The intention in this is that His great Name will cut him off, for he profanes His holy Name and defiles the assembly of Israel, which is consecrated to His Name. And our Sages, of blessed memory, already said this in the Midrash: whoever derives benefit from this world without a blessing is as though he robs the Holy One, blessed be He, and the assembly of Israel, as it is said (Proverbs 28:24): ״גוזל אביו ואמו ואומר אין פשע חבר הוא לאיש משחית״ — 'He who robs his father and his mother and says, it is no transgression, is a companion to a man of destruction.' And 'his father' is none other than the Holy One, blessed be He, and 'his mother' is none other than the assembly of Israel, and this is (Isaiah 50:1): ״ובפשעיכם שלחה אמכם״ — 'and for your transgressions your mother was sent away.' 'A companion to a man of destruction' — he is a companion to Jeroboam, who destroyed Israel from their Father in heaven. How much more so one who offers to Molech the fruit of his body, who causes the Shekhinah to depart from Israel. And it says: למען טמא את מקדשי ולחלל את שם קדשי — 'that he may defile My sanctuary and profane My holy Name,' for he defiles the assembly of Israel, which is consecrated to His Name, and he profanes His great Name. Thus far the language of the Rav. And he explained to us through his words that the word 'I' (אני) in most places of the Torah is the attribute of justice (middat ha-din), but in this place it is the attribute of mercy (middat ha-rahamim), because He mentioned 'My face' (פני); and likewise in the matter of the flood (Genesis 6:17): ״ואני הנני מביא את המבול״ — 'and I, behold I, bring the flood,' and at the beginning of Va'era (Exodus 6:5): ״וגם אני שמעתי״ — 'and I also have heard' — each one is explained and learned from its own context."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:22.112366153Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}